comments.body:
**Preliminary obligation.** Before any interpretation is defined, the solution must pin down, character by character:
- simple types $\sigma::=o\mid\sigma\to\tau$ and the typing rules for $\Lambda_\to$;
- capture-avoiding substitution $M[x:=N]$ on $\alpha$-equivalence classes;
- $\to_\beta$ as the **compatible (congruence) closure** of $(\lambda x.M)N\to M[x:=N]$ — i.e. full $\beta$, redex anywhere, NOT weak/head reduction.

**Why this is a real gate.** The hardest sub-lemma (G6) exists *only* because $\to_\beta$ is the full congruence closure. A solution that silently restricts to head/leftmost reduction would prove weak normalization or a weaker statement, not the asked-for property over all of $\Lambda_\to$. Also: the map is required on all of $\Lambda_\to$ (open terms included), so the interpretation must handle free variables via environments (see G7).
